    <div class="about-page">
      <h2>Why this stuff matters</h2>
      <p>All life on Earth shares a common ancestor, so we share far more genes with other organisms than most people expect — even distant ones like <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drosophila_melanogaster" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">flies</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saccharomyces_cerevisiae" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">yeast</a>. Many genes involved in human disease have direct counterparts in these organisms, and because they're cheap and fast to work with, scientists use them to study disease and test treatments. The connections extend into medicine, agriculture, conservation, and more.</p>

      <h3>Medicine and disease</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Drug discovery — every new drug must be tested in living organisms before human trials. Phylogeny guides which ones: mice share many disease genes with us, so nearly every approved drug was tested in rodents first. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zebrafish" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zebrafish</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caenorhabditis_elegans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>C. elegans</em> worms</a>, and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drosophila_melanogaster" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">fruit flies</a> are also used for early screening because they breed fast and share key disease pathways with humans</li>
        <li>Cancer — two-thirds of genes implicated in human cancers have counterparts in <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6405444/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">fruit flies</a>. Nobel Prize-winning research on the cell cycle was done in <a href="https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/l-h-hartwell-s-yeast-a-model-808/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">yeast</a>, and programmed cell death was discovered in <em>C. elegans</em> worms</li>
        <li>Medical safety — <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horseshoe_crab" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">horseshoe crabs</a> (more closely related to spiders than true crabs) have blue blood with a clotting agent used for decades to test vaccines and medical equipment for bacterial contamination</li>
        <li>Metagenomics — phylogenetics classifies <a href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/gut-bacteria-gene-complement-d/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the thousands of microbial species</a> in a gut microbiome sample</li>
        <li>Comparative genomics — <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature13992" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">comparing human and mouse genomes</a> reveals DNA sequences that control gene expression and would otherwise be nearly impossible to identify</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Genetic tools</h3>
      <p>Many of our most powerful genetic tools were adapted from mechanisms that evolved in other organisms.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>CRISPR — a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CRISPR" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">bacterial immune system</a> repurposed to precisely edit human genes. The FDA <a href="https://www.statnews.com/2023/12/08/fda-approves-casgevy-crispr-based-medicine-for-treatment-of-sickle-cell-disease/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">approved the first CRISPR therapy</a> for sickle cell disease, with trials ongoing for cancers and inherited blindness</li>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RNA_interference" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RNA interference</a> — discovered in <em>C. elegans</em> worms (<a href="https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2006/press-release/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nobel Prize 2006</a>), now the basis of approved drugs that silence disease-causing genes</li>
        <li>Transposon engineering — "jumping genes," first discovered by Barbara McClintock in corn, now harnessed to insert therapeutic genes into human cells. The <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41434-021-00254-w" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sleeping Beauty transposon</a> was reconstructed from ancient fish genomes for use in cancer immunotherapy</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Agriculture and environment</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Crop improvement — <a href="https://daily.jstor.org/can-crops-wild-relatives-save-troubled-agriculture/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">wild relatives</a> of wheat, rice, and tomato are crossed with cultivated varieties to introduce disease resistance and stress tolerance. <a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13059-020-02224-8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pangenome projects</a> now sequence dozens of wild relatives to catalog the full genetic diversity of a crop, revealing genes lost during domestication that breeders can reintroduce</li>
        <li>Conservation — the <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2561-9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">tuatara</a>, sole survivor of its order, is prioritized because it represents a unique evolutionary lineage</li>
        <li>Coevolution — <a href="https://insideecology.com/2025/12/02/figs-and-their-wasps-a-75-million-year-partnership-that-may-be-the-first-known-plant-animal-holobiont/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">figs and fig wasps</a> have coevolved for over 75 million years; Darwin predicted a moth with a foot-long tongue must exist to pollinate a deep orchid — it was found decades later</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Evolutionary origins</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Endosymbiosis — <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endosymbiotic_theory" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mitochondria were originally free-living bacteria</a> that merged with our ancestors ~2 billion years ago; chloroplasts in plant cells have the same origin, descending from a cyanobacterium that was engulfed and never left</li>
        <li>Asgard archaea — the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asgard_archaea" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">discovery of Asgard archaea</a> in deep-sea sediments suggests that eukaryotes — all organisms with a cell nucleus, including us — evolved from within the archaeal domain, collapsing the traditional three-domain tree of life into two</li>
        <li>Viral origins — one hypothesis proposes that the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viral_eukaryogenesis" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eukaryotic nucleus itself originated from a large DNA virus</a> that infected an archaeal host, meaning complex life may partly owe its existence to a viral infection that never resolved</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Biotechnology and discovery</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>PCR — <a href="https://www.nps.gov/articles/thermophile-yell.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Taq polymerase</a>, from heat-tolerant bacteria in Yellowstone hot springs, enabled PCR — a technique that amplifies tiny DNA samples for detection and analysis, now used throughout modern biology and medicine</li>
        <li>Ancestral reconstruction — scientists can <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nrg1324" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">resurrect ancient proteins</a> to study how they evolved, and engineer more stable versions for <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nbt.3677" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">drug development</a> and <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41929-018-0159-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">industrial applications</a></li>
        <li>Convergent evolution — <a href="https://www.science.org/content/article/bats-and-dolphins-evolved-echolocation-same-way" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">bats and dolphins independently evolved echolocation through the same gene mutations</a>, revealing which molecular designs are truly optimal and guiding protein engineering and drug design</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Not everything here started with a practical goal. Much of it began as basic curiosity — open-ended inquiry that produced breakthroughs no one anticipated.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
